{"acceptedResponses":["D"],"answer":"D","bank":"amc","batch":"NA","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(45\\)","B":"\\(62\\)","C":"\\(90\\)","D":"\\(110\\)","E":"\\(135\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{"official-solutions/AMC8_2019_Solutions.pdf":{"kind":"official_booklet","sha256":"8db942c896ec9a0e455849b933d9f19a49462f9f72e0d53b7e4b7c250c4d4381"}},"id":"2019_AMC_8_Q16","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["cnm_p111"],"contentHash":"0d20ce21794a651858c4f673149684267f7aabc6bbde14e5d74b36ce820c2bac","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"added-distance-equation","label":"Rational equation in added distance","methodDirectNodeIds":{"added-distance-equation":["cnm_166","cnm_165"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_166","cnm_165"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"added-time-equation","label":"Linear equation in added time","methodDirectNodeIds":{"added-time-equation":["cnm_043"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_043"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"target-pace-deficit","label":"Recover the deficit against the target pace","methodDirectNodeIds":{"target-pace-deficit":["cnm_047","cnm_p110"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_047","cnm_p110"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":[],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 8","number":16,"officialSolution":{"attribution":"Mathematical Association of America (MAA)","label":"2019 AMC 8","license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","page":10,"sourceURL":"https://drive.usercontent.google.com/uc?id=1LNwtmi2U96Fqq23aquyLcXk96BpgcqF4&export=download","url":"../amc-bank/official-solutions/AMC8_2019_Solutions.pdf"},"question":"Qiang drives \\(15\\) miles at an average speed of \\(30\\) miles per hour. How many additional miles will he have to drive at \\(55\\) miles per hour to average \\(50\\) miles per hour for the entire trip?","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"Compare Qiang's progress with a trip at the desired \\(50\\) mph. His first \\(15\\) miles take \\(15/30=\\frac12\\) hour. In that time, a \\(50\\)-mph trip would cover \\(25\\) miles, so he is \\(10\\) miles behind that target.\n\nDuring the second part, he travels at \\(55\\) mph, gaining \\(55-50=5\\) miles per hour on the target pace. It takes \\(10/5=2\\) hours to make up the \\(10\\)-mile deficit. The additional distance is therefore\n\n\\[\n55\\cdot2=\\boxed{110}\\text{ miles},\n\\]\n\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\). As a check, the full trip covers \\(15+110=125\\) miles in \\(\\frac12+2=2.5\\) hours, giving \\(125/2.5=50\\) mph.","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["cnm_047","cnm_p110"],"explanation":"Compare Qiang's progress with a trip at the desired \\(50\\) mph. His first \\(15\\) miles take \\(15/30=\\frac12\\) hour. In that time, a \\(50\\)-mph trip would cover \\(25\\) miles, so he is \\(10\\) miles behind that target.\n\nDuring the second part, he travels at \\(55\\) mph, gaining \\(55-50=5\\) miles per hour on the target pace. It takes \\(10/5=2\\) hours to make up the \\(10\\)-mile deficit. The additional distance is therefore\n\n\\[\n55\\cdot2=\\boxed{110}\\text{ miles},\n\\]\n\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\). As a check, the full trip covers \\(15+110=125\\) miles in \\(\\frac12+2=2.5\\) hours, giving \\(125/2.5=50\\) mph.","id":"target-pace-deficit","label":"Make up the distance deficit relative to the target speed","legacyRouteId":"target-pace-deficit","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/official-annotations-2026-11-06/2019_AMC_8_Q16.json","sha256":"06c5d406635676042bfb944c5e8943190e32205f849e081654b21409db201715"},{"label":"Final applied official annotation review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/official-annotations-2026-11-06/2019_AMC_8_Q16.json","sha256":"06c5d406635676042bfb944c5e8943190e32205f849e081654b21409db201715"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/b